# M-A: the multiplayer bridge. Requires THREE helpers running:
#
#   docker rm -f aa-server ; docker run -d --name aa-server -p 4534:4534/udp aa-dedicated
#   node bridge/relay.mjs --port 8010 --allow-private > /tmp/relay.log 2>&1 &
#   python3 -m http.server 8008 --directory web/dist-m1 &
#   node web/tools/drive-browser.mjs --headed --out /tmp/bridge-gate \
#        --url 'http://localhost:8008/armagetronad.html?bridge=ws://127.0.0.1:8010' \
#        --script-file web/tools/bridge-gate.steps
#   docker logs aa-server > /tmp/bridge-gate/server.log
#   kill %1 %2      # and LEAVE the container: --rm would delete the log
#
# NO --rm, DELIBERATELY. It deletes the corpse of a crashed server and the log
# with it, which is what happened to Task 3 once -- see
# bridge/test-server/README.md. Remove the previous container by name instead.
#
# OR JUST RUN THE WHOLE THING: web/tools/run-bridge-loss-arm.sh does the
# container, the relay, this file, the B5 kill and the log collection in one
# go, for a clean relay or a lossy one. It leaves NO relay running afterwards,
# because B5's whole point is that the relay is dead.
#
# The container is Task 3's addition and the section further down headed
# "THE REST OF THIS FILE NEEDS A THIRD HELPER" says how to build it. B0, B1
# and A1 do not need it; B2 and B3 are nothing without it.
#
# 127.0.0.1 AND NOT localhost, IN THE ?bridge= VALUE. bridge/relay.mjs binds
# 127.0.0.1 on IPv4 only. A browser that resolves `localhost` to ::1 first gets
# ECONNREFUSED and reports it as a WebSocket error, which reads exactly like a
# bridge defect and is not one. The page's own origin may stay `localhost`; it
# is the ws:// URL the game dials that has to be numeric.
#
# The URL must carry ?bridge= and nothing else. ?autostart=0 holds main() and
# the boot never completes -- that trap has cost two milestones already.
#
# WHY THIS IS A MENU WALK AND NOT JUST A BOOT. Booting with ?bridge= opens
# nothing, and that is correct, not a failure: the client asks for a UDP socket
# only when it enters nCLIENT or nSERVER state (nNetwork.cpp, sn_SetNetState),
# and single-player never does. An earlier draft of this gate waited for
# "[BRIDGE] open" straight after boot; it can only time out, and because
# `until:` deliberately does not throw, the PASS line after it would still have
# printed PASS. So the walk is part of the gate, and B0 below asserts the
# nothing-yet state explicitly rather than leaving it implied.
#
# WHY LAN AND NOT ONLINE MULTIPLAYER. Both open a socket. LAN is the hermetic
# one: nServerInfo::GetFromLAN enters nCLIENT, broadcasts (which the browser
# build refuses -- there is no broadcast transport in a page), waits 1.5 s and
# drops back to nSTANDALONE. No DNS, no master server, no traffic leaving the
# machine, so the only thing this gate can be measuring is the bridge.
#
# THE ROUTE IS ENTERS AND ONE DOWN, READ OFF THE MENU CONSTRUCTORS. uMenu
# clamps its initial `selected` to items.Len()-1 and renders the highest index
# at the top, so each menu opens on its LAST-CONSTRUCTED item and Down moves to
# the previous one -- the same fact web/tools/https-multiplayer.steps documents
# at length:
#   MainMenu  (gGame.cpp, ingame=false)  opens on the Play Game submenu
#   game_menu (same function)            opens on connect, "Multiplayer"
#   net_menu  (net_game(), gGame.cpp)    is cust, mas, fav, bud, opt, lan,
#       inter -- so it opens on inter ("Online Multiplayer") and ONE Down
#       lands on lan ("LAN Multiplayer"), gServerBrowser::BrowseLAN.
# If an item is ever added to any of those three, this script walks somewhere
# else and cannot tell. Read shots 04-06, do not trust the Enter count.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE MENU PATH TO A BOOKMARKED SERVER. Recorded because it is a fact about
# this build, and rediscovering it by trial and error is how gates rot. Read
# off the constructors first and then confirmed screen by screen with a
# throwaway driver run that shot every level (Down 1..4 and the bookmark list
# separately); the screens are named below so a future reader checks the
# shots rather than trusting the count.
#
#   net_menu (net_game(), src/tron/gGame.cpp:2397) constructs, in order:
#       cust, mas, fav, bud, opt, lan, inter
#   and uMenu renders the highest index at the top and opens on the LAST
#   constructed item, so top to bottom the screen reads
#       Online Multiplayer   (inter)   <- where the menu opens
#       LAN Multiplayer      (lan)     <- where the walk above left it
#       Network Setup        (opt)
#       Mates                (bud)
#       Server Bookmarks     (fav)     <- where we are going
#       Subcultures          (mas)
#       Custom Connect       (cust)
#       Exit Menu                      (uMenu's own, always last on screen)
#
#   FROM THE TOP that is Down x4. This script arrives with `lan` still
#   selected, because the LAN walk above entered it and came back and
#   net_menu keeps its `selected` across a submenu, so from HERE it is
#   Down x3. Those two numbers are both correct and are not the same number;
#   shot 10 is what says which situation the run is actually in.
#
#   Then Enter opens gServerFavorites::FavoritesMenu, and the menu that
#   appears reads
#       Connect to 127.0.0.1   <- SELECTED, so no Down at all
#       Edit Bookmarks
#   so ONE MORE Enter connects. Why the bookmark is there and why it is
#   already selected is in web/webdefaults/autoexec.cfg next to
#   CUSTOM_SERVER_NAME: opening this menu runs sg_TransferCustomServer(),
#   AddFavorite fills the HIGHEST empty slot (9, not 0), and
#   sg_GenerateConnectionItems walks 9..0 so slot 9's item is constructed
#   last -- which is the item uMenu opens on. "Connect to 127.0.0.1" is the
#   $bookmarks_menu_connect template with the slot's name_, and name_ is
#   nServerInfoBase::DoGetName(), which for a bookmark is its connection
#   name; that is why the address shows up as the label with nothing typed.
#
# WHY NOT CUSTOM CONNECT, THE MENU THAT LOOKS RIGHT FOR THIS. Its address
# field is a uMenuItemString: reaching a server through it means TYPING an
# address, and the driver's vocabulary is key:NAME -- there is no step that
# enters text. The bookmark route needs only Enter and Down, which is why
# CUSTOM_SERVER_NAME's legacy transfer is the mechanism this gate uses.
#
# AND WHY THERE IS NO ?connect= OR CONSOLE COMMAND. The refusal that keeps a
# bridge-less page alive lives at the head of net_game() -- the single menu
# door, see web/tools/bridge-absent-gate.steps for the two fix rounds it
# cost. Any path that opened a socket without passing through that door would
# put the crash back for every visitor who has no ?bridge=. So the gate walks
# the menus, and that is a requirement, not an inconvenience.
# ===========================================================================
#
# WHY THE RELOAD. Same reason as https-multiplayer.steps: a fresh profile has
# an empty IndexedDB, so boot 1 is the first-use flow and only boot 2 reaches
# the real main menu.
#
# A1 INJECTS ITS OWN DATAGRAMS, AND THAT IS THE POINT. Nothing on this machine
# speaks the game's protocol, so a real reply cannot be provoked -- but the
# queue in web/library_bridge.js is the only way a datagram reaches C++, and
# the relay is the only other thing that writes to it. Pushing two entries onto
# a live handle's queue from here is therefore the same event, and it exercises
# the real eWebNet::Recv in the real build.
#
# What it pins down is the bug the first cut of this task shipped: Recv mapped
# EVERY address text through FakeAddressFor, so a dotted quad came back as a
# fresh synthetic 10.42.0.N while nAddress::SetHostname had stored the real
# address for the same literal. nAddress::Compare then failed and a logged-in
# client silently dropped every packet from its own server. Custom Connect and
# the master listings are both IP literals, so Task 3 would have met it at
# once, as a mysterious no-response rather than an address bug.
#
# THE VERDICT, which is seven lines and not a grep of the whole file:
#   grep -c '\[console.log\].*BRIDGEGATE.*"PASS":true' /tmp/bridge-gate/console.log
# Expected: 7 -- B0, B1, A1 (Task 2), B2, B3 (Task 3) and B4, B5 (Task 4). It was 3 before
# Task 3 appended the two server gates; if a run of the first three alone is
# what is wanted, that number is still 3. Count the [console.log] lines only
# -- the `[harness] until` echoes repeat every needle, so a raw grep
# over-counts roughly threefold. /tmp/relay.log is the far end's half of the
# same claim and must show a `bind` and the port it answered with for each.
#
# AND, FOR B2/B3 ONLY, ONE MORE GREP THAT IS NOT OPTIONAL:
#   grep -iE 'Received login|entered the game' /tmp/bridge-gate/server.log
# The five PASS lines above are all statements the client makes about itself.
# This one is the server saying a datagram arrived from somewhere and it gave
# that somewhere a player. Without it the run has not been checked.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# B6 IS NOT IN THIS FILE, DELIBERATELY. It is web/tools/bridge-gate-b6.steps,
# driven by web/tools/run-bridge-b6.sh, and it could not live here for two
# reasons that file states in full: B5 at the end of this one KILLS THE RELAY
# and has to stay last, so anything appended after it would run with no
# transport at all and could only time out -- and `until:` does not throw, so
# the PASS line after it would print anyway. And B6 dials a community server,
# which means an UNCOMMITTED edit to CUSTOM_SERVER_NAME in
# web/webdefaults/autoexec.cfg; this gate's address stays 127.0.0.1 and the
# repo never ships anyone else's.
#
# B6 also carries an instrument this file does not have: an ack-paired ROUND
# TRIP, recovered from the wire the way nWaitForAck::Ackt recovers it
# (src/network/nNetwork.cpp:846). The gap sampler further down measures how
# often the far end SPEAKS, which is a different quantity and cannot price a
# real network -- on loopback and across a continent it can read the same.
# See docs/evidence/m-a-bridge/README.md section 5.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

until:1:90000:[PERSIST] populate ok
wait:1000
until:1:120000:[BOOT] autostart
wait:9000
shot:01-boot1-language-menu
key:Enter
wait:3000
shot:02-boot1-first-setup
key:Escape
until:1:30000:[PERSISTSAVE] menu-leave
wait:3000
mark:RELOAD-SO-BOOT-2-STARTS-AT-THE-REAL-MAIN-MENU
eval:location.reload()
until:2:90000:[PERSIST] populate ok
until:2:120000:[BOOT] autostart
wait:12000
shot:03-boot2-main-menu

# The peer-mapping lines A1 reads are console.log lines from
# eWebNet::Recv, so they have to be captured as they happen.
eval:(()=>{const o=console.log;if(console.log.__aa)return 'already';window.__peers=[];const w=function(){try{const s=String(arguments[0]);if(s.indexOf('[BRIDGE] peer ')===0)window.__peers.push(s)}catch(e){}return o.apply(console,arguments)};w.__aa=true;console.log=w;return 'peer-line tap installed'})()

# ===========================================================================
# B0: CONFIGURED, AND IDLE UNTIL THE GAME ASKS.
#
# AABridge is the JS library's own state object (web/library_bridge.js), a
# global because the module is not MODULARIZEd -- so this reads the bridge
# rather than reading the console about the bridge. At the main menu, with
# ?bridge= on the URL, the correct state is: no WebSocket constructed, and the
# URL not even parsed yet, because getUrl() is first called from C++ inside
# eWebNet::Create(). "Nothing is attempted" is a claim about this object.
# ===========================================================================
eval:(()=>{const b=window.AABridge;const r={present:!!b,ws_constructed:!!(b&&b.ws),url_parsed:b?b.urlChecked:null,url:b?b.url:null,queues:b?Object.keys(b.queues).length:null,bound:b?Object.keys(b.bound).length:null};r.PASS=!!b&&!r.ws_constructed&&r.url_parsed===false;console.log('[BRIDGEGATE] B0 configured-and-idle-until-asked '+JSON.stringify(r));return 'B0 '+r.PASS})()

mark:ENTER-1-PLAY-GAME-SUBMENU
key:Enter
wait:2500
shot:04-play-game-menu-opens-on-multiplayer
mark:ENTER-2-MULTIPLAYER-SUBMENU
key:Enter
wait:2500
shot:05-network-menu-opens-on-online-multiplayer
mark:ONE-DOWN-TO-LAN-MULTIPLAYER
key:Down
wait:1500
shot:06-network-menu-lan-selected
mark:ENTER-3-LAN-BROWSE-ASKS-FOR-A-SOCKET
key:Enter
until:1:60000:[BRIDGE] open
until:1:60000:bound to udp port
wait:2000
shot:07-lan-browser

# ===========================================================================
# B1: THE SOCKET IS OPEN AND ONE BIND/BOUND ROUND TRIP HAS COMPLETED.
#
# Four readings, all from AABridge: the URL it dialled, its own state flag,
# the live WebSocket.readyState (1 = OPEN, the browser's opinion rather than
# ours), and the bound map -- one entry per handle, 1 meaning the relay
# answered BOUND, -1 meaning it refused. eWebNet::Bind blocks until one of
# those arrives or five seconds pass, so an entry of 0 here would mean the
# handle is still pending, which cannot happen after Bind returned.
#
# The relay's own log is the other half and is NOT checkable from in here;
# /tmp/relay.log must show the matching bind and the udp port it chose.
# ===========================================================================
eval:(()=>{const b=window.AABridge;const bound=Object.keys(b.bound).map(h=>[Number(h),b.bound[h]]);const r={url:b.url,state:b.state,ws_readyState:b.ws?b.ws.readyState:null,bound:bound,pending_datagrams:Object.keys(b.queues).reduce((n,h)=>n+b.queues[h].length,0)};r.PASS=b.state===1&&r.ws_readyState===1&&bound.length>0&&bound.every(e=>e[1]===1);console.log('[BRIDGEGATE] B1 socket-open-and-bound '+JSON.stringify(r));return 'B1 '+r.PASS})()

# ===========================================================================
# A1: AN ADDRESS TEXT IS MAPPED THE WAY THE GAME ITSELF MAPS ONE.
#
# Two datagrams onto the live handle's queue, one from a dotted quad and one
# from a hostname, and then the mapping eWebNet::Recv chose for each. The
# literal must map to ITSELF, because nAddress::SetHostname's numeric branch
# stores the real address for the same text and nAddress::Compare compares the
# 32-bit value. The hostname must map into 10.42.0.0/16, because a page cannot
# resolve it. `drained` being 0 is what says C++ actually consumed them rather
# than the queue simply sitting there.
# ===========================================================================
mark:A1-INJECT-TWO-DATAGRAMS-ON-THE-LIVE-HANDLE
eval:(()=>{const b=window.AABridge;const h=Object.keys(b.queues)[0];if(h===undefined)return 'NO LIVE HANDLE';window.__h=h;const push=(a)=>b.queues[h].push({addr:a,port:4534,bytes:new Uint8Array([0,0,0,0])});push('127.0.0.1');push('tron.example.org');return 'injected 2 datagrams on handle '+h})()
until:1:30000:[BRIDGE] peer 127.0.0.1 -> 127.0.0.1 (literal)
until:1:30000:[BRIDGE] peer tron.example.org -> 10.42.
wait:1500
eval:(()=>{const p=window.__peers||[];const lit=p.filter(l=>l.indexOf('peer 127.0.0.1 -> 127.0.0.1 (literal)')>=0).length;const syn=p.filter(l=>/peer tron\.example\.org -> 10\.42\.[0-9.]+ \(synthetic\)/.test(l)).length;const q=(window.AABridge.queues[window.__h]||[]);const r={peer_lines:p,literal_maps_to_itself:lit,hostname_maps_to_synthetic:syn,drained:q.length,dropped:window.AABridge.dropped|0,send_failures:window.AABridge.sendFailures|0};r.PASS=lit===1&&syn===1&&r.drained===0;console.log('[BRIDGEGATE] A1 address-text-maps-the-way-the-game-maps-it '+JSON.stringify(r));return 'A1 '+r.PASS})()
shot:08-after-the-injected-datagrams

mark:BACK-OUT-AND-CONFIRM-THE-PAGE-IS-STILL-ALIVE
key:Escape
wait:3000
shot:09-back-in-the-network-menu
eval:(()=>{const c=document.getElementById('canvas');const g=c.getContext('webgl');return 'alive, gl err=0x'+(g?g.getError().toString(16):'nogl')})()

# ===========================================================================
# THE REST OF THIS FILE NEEDS A THIRD HELPER: A REAL SERVER.
#
#   docker build -f bridge/test-server/Dockerfile -t aa-dedicated .   # repo root
#   docker rm -f aa-server ; docker run -d --name aa-server -p 4534:4534/udp aa-dedicated
#   ... run the driver as at the top of this file ...
#   docker logs aa-server > /tmp/bridge-gate/server.log
#   docker rm -f aa-server
#
# Everything above this line runs without it and still passes; B2 and B3
# below cannot, and they are the milestone's actual gate. See
# bridge/test-server/README.md.
#
# IF B2 TIMES OUT, CHECK THE CONTAINER IS STILL ALIVE BEFORE SUSPECTING THE
# BRIDGE. It happened once while this gate was being written: every reading in
# the page was correct -- WebSocket open, a handle bound, the relay logging the
# udp port -- and the connect simply got no answer, because `aa-server` had
# exited part way through the run and `--rm` had already deleted it, log and
# all. `docker ps -a` said "No such container". Start it WITHOUT `--rm` when
# investigating, so the corpse and its log survive to be read:
#   docker run -d --name aa-server -p 4534:4534/udp aa-dedicated
#   docker inspect -f '{{.State.Status}} {{.State.ExitCode}}' aa-server
# The same run repeated with no --rm passed, and the container stayed up
# through it, so this is not a known failure with a known cause -- which is
# precisely why the first thing to rule out is the server having gone away.
#
# THE NAMES ARE B2 AND B3, NOT B1 AND B2. Task 3's brief called these two
# verdicts B1 and B2, written before Task 2 had used B0/B1/A1 in this same
# file. Renaming Task 2's B1 to free the name would have falsified the labels
# in docs/evidence/m-a-bridge/task2/, which is worse than a gap in the
# numbering, so the new verdicts continue the sequence instead.
#
# THE VERDICT FOR THE WHOLE FILE IS SEVEN AS OF TASK 4, WAS FIVE, NEVER THREE:
#   grep -c '\[console.log\].*BRIDGEGATE.*"PASS":true' /tmp/bridge-gate/console.log
# Expected: 7 -- B0, B1, A1, B2, B3, B4, B5. It was 5 before Task 4 appended
# the loss and dropped-connection verdicts, and 3 before Task 3 appended the
# two server gates; each of those numbers was right for the file it described.
#
# The [L] ladder-log lines B2 and B3 read are console.log lines from
# se_SaveToLadderLog (CONSOLE_LADDER_LOG 1, set in autoexec.cfg), so they have
# to be counted as they happen, the same way the peer tap above works. The tap
# only COUNTS -- it must not re-log, or the `until:` counts below would double.
eval:(()=>{if(window.__lTap)return 'already';window.__lTap=true;window.__L={};const o=console.log;const w=function(){try{const s=String(arguments[0]);if(s.indexOf('[L] ')===0){const k=s.slice(4).split(' ')[0];window.__L[k]=(window.__L[k]||0)+1}}catch(e){}return o.apply(console,arguments)};console.log=w;return 'ladder-log tap installed'})()

# AND A MILESTONE TICKER, BECAUSE THE `[L]` NEEDLES BELOW USED TO BE COUNTING
# THE WRONG GAME. This is a correction to Task 3 and it was found by re-running
# its own gate, which reported B3 PASS:false on a healthy run.
#
# BOOT 1 PLAYS A LOCAL GAME. The first-use flow starts one before anybody
# touches a menu, with local AIs, and it emits real ladder-log lines -- measured
# in docs/evidence/m-a-bridge/task4/loss/clean-before-the-ticker/console.log at
# 13.9 s, before the reload:
#     [L] TEAM_PLAYER_ADDED web_user web_user
#     [L] TEAM_PLAYER_ADDED ai_team word / excel / notepad
#     [L] ROUND_SCORE_TEAM 0 web_user
#     [L] NEW_ROUND ...
# drive-browser.mjs's `until:` counts the WHOLE transcript, so every one of
# those is already in the count before the connect happens. The harness said so
# plainly and it was not read:
#     until 1x <<[L] TEAM_PLAYER_ADDED>> (timeout 60000ms, have 4)
#     until 1x <<[L] ROUND_SCORE_TEAM>> (timeout 180000ms, have 1)
#     until 2x <<[L] NEW_ROUND>> (timeout 180000ms, have 2)
# -- all three satisfied on arrival. So the driver walked straight past B3's
# wait and B3's verdict, which counts from the tap and is therefore right, read
# rounds_started 1 / rounds_scored 0 and printed PASS:false. The round had not
# happened yet. Task 3's own run passed on the same file, so this is a race it
# won once, not a thing that never worked.
#
# THE FIX IS TO WAIT ON THE TAP INSTEAD OF ON THE TRANSCRIPT. The tap is
# installed above, i.e. after boot 1 and after the reload, so its counts only
# ever see the real server. This ticker turns those counts into needles: each
# milestone is printed ONCE, so `until:1:` on one of them is exact, and no
# count in this file has to be adjusted for how many times an unrelated part of
# the walk happens to start a game.
eval:(()=>{if(window.__gw)return 'already';window.__gw={};window.__gwTimer=setInterval(()=>{const L=window.__L||{};const say=(k)=>{if(!window.__gw[k]){window.__gw[k]=1;console.log('[GATEWAIT] '+k)}};const n=L.NEW_ROUND|0,sc=L.ROUND_SCORE_TEAM|0,ro=L.TEAM_PLAYER_ADDED|0;for(let i=1;i<=8;i++){if(ro>=i)say('ROSTER-'+i);if(n>=i)say('STARTED-'+i);if(sc>=i)say('SCORED-'+i)}},500);return 'gate-wait milestone ticker installed'})()

# AND A BASELINE OF THE ERROR COUNTERS, BECAUSE THEY ARE NOT ZERO HERE AND
# ASKING FOR ZERO WOULD BE AN ASSERTION THAT CAN NEVER PASS. The LAN walk
# above leaves AABridge.dataErrors at 8 on this build, every one of them
#   [BRIDGE] error on handle N: port 0 is outside the allowed range 4533-4599
# and they are NOT the broadcast, which is the obvious guess and is wrong:
# nSocket::Broadcast returns -1 under __EMSCRIPTEN__ && !DEDICATED
# (src/network/nSocket.cpp:2210), so a broadcast never reaches the relay at
# all. They come from the line ABOVE it in nServerInfo::GetFromLAN
# (src/network/nServerInfo.cpp:1649), whose per-port loop over 4534..4540 does
#     m->SendImmediately(0, false);          <- plain unicast to peer 0
#     nMessage::BroadcastCollected(0, port);
# and peer 0's nAddress was never given a port -- the LAN browse has no server
# to point it at -- so the port on the wire is 0 and bridge/policy.mjs refuses
# it against its 4533-4599 range. Correct on both sides, and the bursts land
# exactly at the nSTANDALONE<->nCLIENT transitions (four on handle 1, then
# handle 2 binds; four on handle 2, then handle 3 binds). Four rather than
# seven because the send buffer coalesces the iterations. It means "no errors"
# has to mean "no NEW errors".
# Measured, so the number is a reading and not a guess: 8 before this line,
# 8 at B2, 8 at B3 -- the connect and the whole round added none. A delta is
# also the stronger claim: a fixed `=== 8` would silently accept a real
# datagram error appearing while the eighth broadcast error went away.
eval:(()=>{const b=window.AABridge;window.__base={dropped:b.dropped|0,sendFailures:b.sendFailures|0,dataErrors:b.dataErrors|0};return 'error-counter baseline '+JSON.stringify(window.__base)})()

mark:B2-DOWN-3-FROM-LAN-TO-SERVER-BOOKMARKS
key:Down:3
wait:1500
shot:10-network-menu-server-bookmarks-selected
mark:B2-ENTER-OPENS-BOOKMARKS-AND-TRANSFERS-CUSTOM_SERVER_NAME
key:Enter
wait:3000
shot:11-bookmarks-menu-opens-on-connect-to-127-0-0-1
mark:B2-ENTER-CONNECTS
key:Enter

# THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO `until:...:[BRIDGE] handle` HERE, AND THE FIRST
# DRAFT HAD ONE. It read `until:2:30000:[BRIDGE] handle` on the theory that
# the LAN walk bound one handle and the connect would be the second. Run, the
# harness printed
#     until 2x <<[BRIDGE] handle>> (timeout 30000ms, have 3)
# -- the LAN walk binds TWICE (handles 1 and 2, as sn_SetNetState cycles), so
# the needle was already satisfied before the Enter above did anything, and
# `until:` does not throw, so the verdict after it would have printed PASS on
# a run where the connect never happened. Any fixed count here is a count of
# how many times an unrelated part of the walk happens to rebind, which is
# exactly the kind of number that rots. The two needles below cannot be
# satisfied by anything but a real server, at any count.
#
# The client cannot invent either of them. A team roster and a round number
# come from the server's game state over the wire; nothing local produces them
# -- the walk above never starts a game, and a grep of the transcript up to
# this point finds zero [L] NEW_ROUND. What the socket did is checked in the
# verdict below from AABridge instead, where it is a reading of the live
# handle rather than a count of log lines: exactly one live handle, because
# aa_bridge_close deleted the LAN one's queue, bound and sentSinceBind.
until:1:60000:[GATEWAIT] ROSTER-1
until:1:60000:[GATEWAIT] STARTED-1
wait:4000
shot:12-on-the-grid-of-a-real-network-game

# THE DATAGRAM-GAP SAMPLER, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES A LOSS RUN A MEASUREMENT.
#
# It wraps the LIVE WebSocket's onmessage, so it counts every frame the relay
# delivers to this page and the gap since the previous one. library_bridge.js
# assigns `AABridge.ws.onmessage = AABridge.onmessage` once
# (web/library_bridge.js:152), so wrapping the socket's property leaves the
# bridge's own handler untouched and simply times it; the wrapper calls it and
# swallows nothing.
#
# WHY A GAP DISTRIBUTION AND NOT A MEAN. The question loss raises is not "is
# the average worse" -- it is "how long does the stream stop for". A mean hides
# exactly that, and a 5 % loss run whose mean moved by two milliseconds can
# still have stalled for a second. So the readout below reports p50/p90/p99,
# the maximum, and the counts over 100/250/500 ms, and the report quotes the
# tail rather than the average.
eval:(()=>{try{const b=window.AABridge;if(!b||!b.ws)return 'NO LIVE SOCKET - the sampler measured nothing';if(window.__gapTap)return 'already';window.__gapTap=true;window.__gaps=[];window.__gapLast=0;const inner=b.ws.onmessage;b.ws.onmessage=function(ev){const t=performance.now();if(window.__gapLast)window.__gaps.push(Math.round((t-window.__gapLast)*100)/100);window.__gapLast=t;return inner.apply(this,arguments)};return 'gap sampler installed on the live socket'}catch(e){return 'GAP SAMPLER FAILED: '+e}})()

# ===========================================================================
# B2: THE CLIENT REACHED THE BOOKMARKED SERVER THROUGH THE BRIDGE.
#
# Read from AABridge and from the ladder-log tap, not from the screen. The
# screen saying "connected" is a statement about what the client believes;
# TEAM_PLAYER_ADDED naming a roster and NEW_ROUND naming a round are
# statements about what the server sent.
#
# handles: exactly one live handle, its bound entry 1 (the relay answered
#   BOUND, not ERROR), and sentSinceBind > 0 on it -- datagrams left the page
#   on the handle the game is actually using.
# new_dropped / new_sendFailures / new_dataErrors: all 0, counted as deltas
#   from the baseline taken before the Downs above -- see the comment on that
#   baseline for why an absolute zero is unreachable here. new_dataErrors is
#   the one that would catch the relay refusing the destination by policy,
#   which is what happens without --allow-private and looks like silence
#   otherwise.
# pending_before/pending_after and draining: the queue depth sampled twice,
#   300 ms apart, and the claim is that it is SHALLOW BOTH TIMES -- not that it
#   is empty. The first cut asked for pending === 0 and a re-run of this gate
#   measured 1, with every other field green: one datagram the relay had
#   delivered that C++ had not yet drained, which is a race against the next
#   eWebNet::Recv and not a defect. An instantaneous zero is luck about when
#   the reading landed; a queue that is shallow twice running is the actual
#   claim, and a wedged consumer -- a queue that grows -- fails it, which the
#   zero-check could also do only by luck.
#
# THE ASSERTION THAT MATTERS IS NOT IN HERE AND CANNOT BE. It is
#   docker logs aa-server | grep -iE 'entered|Received login'
# which must name the player and the source address of its datagrams. If the
# server's log and this verdict ever disagree, the server's log wins and the
# client is what is broken.
# ===========================================================================
eval:(async()=>{const b=window.AABridge;const L=window.__L||{};const B=window.__base||{dropped:0,sendFailures:0,dataErrors:0};const live=Object.keys(b.queues).map(Number);const bound=live.map(h=>[h,b.bound[h]===undefined?0:b.bound[h]]);const sent=live.map(h=>[h,b.sentSinceBind[h]|0]);const dep=()=>live.reduce((a,h)=>a+((b.queues[h]||[]).length),0);const p1=dep();await new Promise(rr=>setTimeout(rr,300));const p2=dep();const r={ws_readyState:b.ws?b.ws.readyState:null,state:b.state,live_handles:live,bound:bound,sent:sent,pending_before:p1,pending_after:p2,baseline:B,dropped:b.dropped|0,sendFailures:b.sendFailures|0,dataErrors:b.dataErrors|0,new_dropped:(b.dropped|0)-B.dropped,new_sendFailures:(b.sendFailures|0)-B.sendFailures,new_dataErrors:(b.dataErrors|0)-B.dataErrors,lastDataError:b.lastDataError,rosters:L.TEAM_PLAYER_ADDED|0,rounds_started:L.NEW_ROUND|0};r.draining=p1<=3&&p2<=3;r.PASS=b.state===1&&r.ws_readyState===1&&live.length===1&&bound.every(e=>e[1]===1)&&sent.every(e=>e[1]>0)&&r.draining&&r.new_dropped===0&&r.new_sendFailures===0&&r.new_dataErrors===0&&r.rosters>0&&r.rounds_started>0;console.log('[BRIDGEGATE] B2 reached-the-bookmarked-server-through-the-bridge '+JSON.stringify(r));return 'B2 '+r.PASS})()

mark:B3-STEER-WHILE-A-NETWORK-ROUND-IS-LIVE
key:Left
wait:500
key:Right

# WHY NOT [L] ROUND_WINNER, WHICH IS WHAT THE BRIEF ASKED FOR. This build
# never prints it on a client. It is written from gGame::Analysis
# (src/tron/gGame.cpp:3610), whose first two lines are
#     if ( nCLIENT == sn_GetNetState() )
#         return;
# so the whole round-end analysis, ROUND_WINNER and MATCH_WINNER included, is
# server-only. Measured: a three-round run of this exact walk produced
# 0x ROUND_WINNER and 4x NEW_ROUND in the browser transcript. Waiting for it
# here would have timed out, and `until:` does not throw, so the PASS after
# it would still have printed -- a gate that measured nothing.
#
# TWO NEEDLES INSTEAD, BOTH OF WHICH REQUIRE A ROUND TO HAVE FINISHED:
#
# [L] ROUND_SCORE_TEAM is eTeam::LogScoreDifference (src/engine/eTeam.cpp:564)
#   reached from ePlayerNetID::LogScoreDifferences() in the GS_CREATED /
#   GS_TRANSFER_SETTINGS arm of gGame::StateUpdate (gGame.cpp:3189) -- NOT
#   inside that arm's `if ( nCLIENT != sn_GetNetState() )` block, which is why
#   it fires on a client where ROUND_WINNER does not. It reports
#   `score - lastScore_` for the round that just ENDED and names the player's
#   team, so it is simultaneously "a round completed" and "the browser player
#   was in it, and the server scored it": the score it prints is the server's
#   number, arriving over this socket. Observed: `-2 web_user`, the browser
#   player having been killed by an AI each round.
#
# [L] NEW_ROUND twice is the state machine having gone all the way round. One
#   is what B2 already asserted; a second one cannot happen unless the first
#   round ran to its end. Measured cadence on this machine: NEW_ROUND at
#   41.7s, 54.5s, 67.3s, 80.1s -- one per round, ~13 s apart, so 180 s is
#   many rounds of headroom rather than a lucky fit. Counting is safe here
#   because the harness excludes its own lines from `until:` (drive-browser.mjs
#   countMatches) -- a raw grep of the transcript does NOT, which is what the
#   header's "count the [console.log] lines only" is about.
#
# WHAT THE TWO KEYPRESSES DO AND DO NOT PROVE, stated plainly rather than
# dressed up. They drive the real input path -- uInput to gCycle to a message
# on the same socket the round is running over -- while a network game is
# live, and a path that threw or wedged there would stop the round finishing
# and time these needles out. They do NOT prove a specific turn reached the
# server: neither end logs anything per turn, and a round's duration is not a
# measurement of one keypress. If a later task needs that claim it needs a new
# instrument, not a longer wait here.
until:1:180000:[GATEWAIT] SCORED-1
until:1:180000:[GATEWAIT] STARTED-2
wait:2000
shot:13-a-round-has-completed-with-the-browser-player-scored-in-it

# ===========================================================================
# B3: A FULL ROUND COMPLETED WITH THE BROWSER PLAYER ON THE GRID.
#
# rounds_started >= 2 and rounds_scored >= 1 are the tap's own counts of the
# two needles above, so the verdict does not depend on the harness having
# waited correctly. The bridge readings are repeated because a round that
# finished on a socket that has since died would not be the claim being made:
# the same handle must still be the only live one, still bound, still sending,
# with nothing newly dropped (deltas again, same baseline as B2) and nothing
# left undrained. `sent` is worth reading rather than only asserting: it was
# 73 datagrams at B2 and 206 at B3 on the measured run, i.e. the round is a
# live two-way stream on this handle and not a login followed by silence.
#
# still_alive is the port's standing check -- the canvas has a size and GL
# reports no error -- because every reading above is compatible with a module
# that has since exited.
# ===========================================================================
eval:(async()=>{const b=window.AABridge;const L=window.__L||{};const B=window.__base||{dropped:0,sendFailures:0,dataErrors:0};const c=document.getElementById('canvas');const g=c?c.getContext('webgl'):null;const live=Object.keys(b.queues).map(Number);const bound=live.map(h=>[h,b.bound[h]===undefined?0:b.bound[h]]);const sent=live.map(h=>[h,b.sentSinceBind[h]|0]);const dep=()=>live.reduce((a,h)=>a+((b.queues[h]||[]).length),0);const p1=dep();await new Promise(rr=>setTimeout(rr,300));const p2=dep();const r={rounds_started:L.NEW_ROUND|0,rounds_scored:L.ROUND_SCORE_TEAM|0,rosters:L.TEAM_PLAYER_ADDED|0,ws_readyState:b.ws?b.ws.readyState:null,live_handles:live,bound:bound,sent:sent,pending_before:p1,pending_after:p2,baseline:B,new_dropped:(b.dropped|0)-B.dropped,new_sendFailures:(b.sendFailures|0)-B.sendFailures,new_dataErrors:(b.dataErrors|0)-B.dataErrors,canvas_w:c?c.width:0,canvas_h:c?c.height:0,gl_err:g?g.getError():'nogl'};r.still_alive=r.canvas_w>0&&r.canvas_h>0&&r.gl_err===0;r.draining=p1<=3&&p2<=3;r.PASS=r.rounds_started>=2&&r.rounds_scored>=1&&r.ws_readyState===1&&live.length===1&&bound.every(e=>e[1]===1)&&sent.every(e=>e[1]>0)&&r.draining&&r.new_dropped===0&&r.new_sendFailures===0&&r.new_dataErrors===0&&r.still_alive;console.log('[BRIDGEGATE] B3 a-full-round-completed-with-the-browser-player-scored-in-it '+JSON.stringify(r));return 'B3 '+r.PASS})()

# The gap readout for the first stretch, so the report has a before as well as
# an after. `datagrams` is the sampler's own count of frames delivered to this
# page: a zero here would mean the wrapper never ran and every percentile below
# it is meaningless, which is why it is printed rather than assumed.
eval:(()=>{const g=(window.__gaps||[]).slice().sort((a,b)=>a-b);const q=p=>g.length?g[Math.min(g.length-1,Math.floor(p*g.length))]:null;const r={phase:'through-B3',datagrams:g.length,p50:q(.5),p90:q(.9),p99:q(.99),max:g.length?g[g.length-1]:null,over100ms:g.filter(x=>x>100).length,over250ms:g.filter(x=>x>250).length,over500ms:g.filter(x=>x>500).length};console.log('[BRIDGEGATE] GAPS '+JSON.stringify(r));return 'gaps '+JSON.stringify(r)})()

# ===========================================================================
# B4: A FURTHER ROUND COMPLETES WHILE DATAGRAMS ARE GOING MISSING.
#
# THE NAMES ARE B4 AND B5, NOT B3 AND B4. Task 4's brief called these two
# verdicts B3 and B4, written before Task 3 had used B2/B3 in this same file.
# Renaming Task 3's would falsify the labels in docs/evidence/m-a-bridge/b1
# and b2, so the sequence continues instead -- the same reason Task 3 gave for
# not reusing B1.
#
# AND IT IS NOT `[L] ROUND_WINNER`, WHICH IS WHAT THE BRIEF ASKED FOR. This
# build never prints that on a client: gGame::Analysis returns immediately
# when sn_GetNetState() == nCLIENT (src/tron/gGame.cpp:3610), so ROUND_WINNER
# and MATCH_WINNER are server-only. Task 3 measured it -- a three-round run
# produced 0x ROUND_WINNER -- and `until:` does not throw, so a gate written
# on that needle would have timed out and then printed PASS anyway. The two
# needles below are Task 3's, which a client does print, with the counts moved
# on by one round.
#
# WHAT THIS VERDICT DOES AND DOES NOT CLAIM. It claims that a further full
# round completed on a socket that is still the only live one, still bound,
# still sending, with nothing newly dropped and nothing left undrained. It
# does NOT claim by itself that loss was happening: the relay discards
# silently and NOTHING INSIDE THIS PAGE CAN SEE A DATAGRAM THAT NEVER ARRIVED.
#
# The three error deltas are all asserted zero, new_dataErrors included. The
# first cut of this verdict omitted new_dataErrors while B2 and B3 both require
# it, which would have let the relay start refusing destinations by policy --
# what happens without --allow-private, and what looks like silence from
# anywhere else -- without B4 noticing. Both arms measured 0, so adding it
# changed no result; it was a hole in the check, not a wrong reading.
# That half of the claim is the relay's own log, which must show
#   [bridge] discarded N datagrams so far (--drop 0.05)
# and is checked by web/tools/run-bridge-loss-arm.sh, not from in here. Run
# with a clean relay this section passes identically, and that is not a defect
# in it -- the measurement is the COMPARISON of the two runs, which is in
# docs/evidence/m-a-bridge/README.md.
# ===========================================================================
mark:B4-A-FURTHER-ROUND-WITH-DATAGRAMS-GOING-MISSING
until:1:240000:[GATEWAIT] SCORED-2
until:1:240000:[GATEWAIT] STARTED-3
wait:2000
shot:13b-a-further-round-completed
eval:(async()=>{const b=window.AABridge;const L=window.__L||{};const B=window.__base||{dropped:0,sendFailures:0,dataErrors:0};const c=document.getElementById('canvas');const g=c?c.getContext('webgl'):null;const live=Object.keys(b.queues).map(Number);const bound=live.map(h=>[h,b.bound[h]===undefined?0:b.bound[h]]);const sent=live.map(h=>[h,b.sentSinceBind[h]|0]);const dep=()=>live.reduce((a,h)=>a+((b.queues[h]||[]).length),0);const p1=dep();await new Promise(rr=>setTimeout(rr,300));const p2=dep();const r={rounds_started:L.NEW_ROUND|0,rounds_scored:L.ROUND_SCORE_TEAM|0,ws_readyState:b.ws?b.ws.readyState:null,live_handles:live,bound:bound,sent:sent,pending_before:p1,pending_after:p2,new_dropped:(b.dropped|0)-B.dropped,new_sendFailures:(b.sendFailures|0)-B.sendFailures,new_dataErrors:(b.dataErrors|0)-B.dataErrors,canvas_w:c?c.width:0,canvas_h:c?c.height:0,gl_err:g?g.getError():'nogl'};r.still_alive=r.canvas_w>0&&r.canvas_h>0&&r.gl_err===0;r.draining=p1<=3&&p2<=3;r.PASS=r.rounds_started>=3&&r.rounds_scored>=2&&r.ws_readyState===1&&live.length===1&&bound.every(e=>e[1]===1)&&sent.every(e=>e[1]>0)&&r.draining&&r.new_dropped===0&&r.new_sendFailures===0&&r.new_dataErrors===0&&r.still_alive;console.log('[BRIDGEGATE] B4 a-further-round-with-datagrams-going-missing '+JSON.stringify(r));return 'B4 '+r.PASS})()

# The gap readout for the whole session. This is the number the loss table in
# docs/evidence/m-a-bridge/README.md is built from.
eval:(()=>{const g=(window.__gaps||[]).slice().sort((a,b)=>a-b);const q=p=>g.length?g[Math.min(g.length-1,Math.floor(p*g.length))]:null;const r={phase:'through-B4',datagrams:g.length,p50:q(.5),p90:q(.9),p99:q(.99),max:g.length?g[g.length-1]:null,over100ms:g.filter(x=>x>100).length,over250ms:g.filter(x=>x>250).length,over500ms:g.filter(x=>x>500).length};console.log('[BRIDGEGATE] GAPS '+JSON.stringify(r));return 'gaps '+JSON.stringify(r)})()

# ===========================================================================
# B5: THE CONNECTION DIES MID-ROUND.
#
# THE RELAY PROCESS IS KILLED FROM THE SHELL, and that is a correction to the
# brief rather than a convenience. The brief closed the socket with
# `eval:AABridge.ws.close()`. That would in fact have worked -- AABridge is a
# page global on this build, and every verdict above reads it -- but a
# scripted close is the client asking politely, and the failure worth testing
# is the far end going away without telling anyone. So
# web/tools/run-bridge-loss-arm.sh waits for the mark below to appear in the
# transcript and then kills the relay, which is a real dropped connection: the
# TCP socket goes away under the page.
#
# THIS MUST BE LAST IN THE FILE. After it there is no relay, so nothing after
# it could mean anything.
#
# WHAT IS BEING ASSERTED. Not "it survived" in the loose sense -- three
# specific things:
#   ws_readyState 3 and state 2: the browser's own opinion of the socket, and
#     the bridge's own flag, which onclose sets (library_bridge.js:151).
#   raf_ticking: two nested requestAnimationFrame callbacks arrived within
#     three seconds. This is the one that catches the failure that matters. A
#     wedged Asyncify stack -- something re-entering C++ from a socket event --
#     leaves the canvas its old size and GL its old state, so a canvas-and-GL
#     check would pass over a module that has stopped running. A module that
#     has stopped running does not paint.
#   no Emscripten abort, which is checked from the transcript by the runner
#     because an abort() kills the page's ability to answer at all.
# ===========================================================================
mark:B5-THE-RELAY-PROCESS-IS-KILLED-FROM-THE-SHELL-DURING-THIS-WAIT
wait:30000
until:1:40000:[BRIDGE] closed
wait:6000
shot:14-after-the-far-end-went-away
eval:(async()=>{const b=window.AABridge;const c=document.getElementById('canvas');const g=c?c.getContext('webgl'):null;const raf=await new Promise(res=>{let done=false;const to=setTimeout(()=>{if(!done)res(false)},3000);requestAnimationFrame(()=>requestAnimationFrame(()=>{done=true;clearTimeout(to);res(true)}))});const r={ws_readyState:b.ws?b.ws.readyState:null,state:b.state,raf_ticking:raf,sendFailures:b.sendFailures|0,dropped:b.dropped|0,canvas_w:c?c.width:0,canvas_h:c?c.height:0,gl_err:g?g.getError():'nogl'};r.PASS=r.ws_readyState===3&&r.state===2&&r.raf_ticking===true&&r.canvas_w>0&&r.canvas_h>0&&r.gl_err===0;console.log('[BRIDGEGATE] B5 the-far-end-went-away-and-the-module-is-still-running '+JSON.stringify(r));return 'B5 '+r.PASS})()
wait:2000
shot:15-still-running-after-the-bridge-died
